<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<Defs>

  <!-- The visible trims, in four families: the floor border, the inlay, the
       vine and the pebbles. Each family has seven shapes: a border strip, its
       corner, the inside corner, the double-rail runner, the end cap, the
       frame and the diagonal. 1×1 non-edifice buildings that dress the floor
       without owning the cell, so they coexist with furniture (only edifices
       wipe edifices) and with any terrain, ours or another mod's. Stuffable
       and paintable.

       Each carries BlocksTerrainFade for the edges its art covers, so a
       decorated strip is also a hard edge: the hardening follows the same
       rules as the invisible marker, two-sided, with corners sealed.

       Each family draws from ONE strip texture, laid out as geometry by
       NeatEdges.Graphic_StripTrim: a band along each edge the shape covers,
       cut at 45 degrees where two meet, mapped to world position along the
       band. Each names its shape with a NeatEdges.TrimPiece, and its strip's
       band count and variants. The strip's first band is the band on a north
       edge and its second the band on a south edge, so west and east arms keep
       the north-west light by taking the matching band; the third is the
       side-lit band a diagonal draws when its wall lies north-east or
       south-west. Until 2026-10 each shape shipped a texture per facing, 21 in
       all, kept out of the static atlas by a Harmony patch; the strips never
       enter the atlas, and every trim in a map section draws in one call
       whatever its family, material or paint. Every def names a uiIconPath:
       without one its build button would show the strip.

       Built on two levels of abstract parents: NE_TrimBase for what every trim
       shares, then one per shape for what that shape is (its edges, cost,
       description and drag). A family's def adds its name, its strip and its
       TrimPiece. modExtensions lists append down the chain, so a shape's
       BlocksTerrainFade and a family's TrimPiece end up side by side.

       The floor border moved here from Fine Establishments (2026-09-24), where
       it began, with its art and generator (devtools/make_trim_art.py); the
       other families are drawn by devtools/make_trim_styles.py. defNames are
       permanent once shipped.

       The border ROTATES to pick its edge rather than linking: a centred
       linked band was built first and rejected in play, because a link mask
       can never say which side its wall is on. -->

  <ThingDef Name="NE_TrimBase" ParentName="BuildingBase" Abstract="True">
    <thingClass>Building</thingClass>
    <graphicData>
      <graphicClass>NeatEdges.Graphic_StripTrim</graphicClass>
      <drawSize>(1,1)</drawSize>
    </graphicData>
    <rotatable>true</rotatable>
    <defaultPlacingRot>South</defaultPlacingRot>
    <altitudeLayer>TerrainScatter</altitudeLayer>
    <passability>Standable</passability>
    <pathCost>0</pathCost>
    <fillPercent>0</fillPercent>
    <selectable>true</selectable>
    <noRightClickDraftAttack>true</noRightClickDraftAttack>
    <destroyable>true</destroyable>
    <clearBuildingArea>false</clearBuildingArea>
    <canOverlapZones>true</canOverlapZones>
    <tradeability>None</tradeability>
    <leaveResourcesWhenKilled>false</leaveResourcesWhenKilled>
    <resourcesFractionWhenDeconstructed>0</resourcesFractionWhenDeconstructed>
    <statBases>
      <Flammability>0.5</Flammability>
    </statBases>
    <stuffCategories>
      <li>Woody</li>
      <li>Stony</li>
      <li>Metallic</li>
    </stuffCategories>
    <terrainAffordanceNeeded>Light</terrainAffordanceNeeded>
    <!-- On the Floors tab, but not as a button each: every shape is one
         button (NeatEdges.Designator_TrimShape), its styles on a right-click
         menu, and each shape's abstract def below names its dropdown group
         (Defs/Misc/NeatEdges_TrimShapes.xml). Still buildable, with blueprints
         and frames, because BuildableByPlayer asks only for a
         designationCategory. -->
    <designationCategory>Floors</designationCategory>
    <canGenerateDefaultDesignator>false</canGenerateDefaultDesignator>
    <constructionSkillPrerequisite>3</constructionSkillPrerequisite>
    <building>
      <isEdifice>false</isEdifice>
      <paintable>true</paintable>
      <ai_chillDestination>false</ai_chillDestination>
      <ai_neverTrashThis>true</ai_neverTrashThis>
      <artificialForMeditationPurposes>false</artificialForMeditationPurposes>
      <canLandGravshipOn>true</canLandGravshipOn>
    </building>
  </ThingDef>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       The shapes
       =================================================================== -->

  <!-- The straight: one band along one edge, running its full length, so a
       row of them reads as one continuous line. Rotation names the hugged
       edge directly, with no offset. -->
  <ThingDef Name="NE_TrimStraightBase" ParentName="NE_TrimBase" Abstract="True">
    <description>A border strip set into the floor along one edge of a tile, running its full length, so a row of them reads as one continuous line. Rotate to choose the edge. The edge it covers stays crisp: terrain stops fading across it.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>40</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>150</WorkToBuild>
      <Beauty>1</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <costStuffCount>2</costStuffCount>
    <designatorDropdown>NE_TrimStraights</designatorDropdown>
    <!-- Line-drag comes from the Defenses draw styles alone;
         placingDraggableDimensions is a dead field in 1.6. -->
    <drawStyleCategory>Defenses</drawStyleCategory>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="NeatEdges.BlocksTerrainFade">
        <!-- Measured, not assumed: decoding the floor border's art puts the
             opaque band in rows 0-34 of a north edge's 256 (the top 14%),
             which is the strip's first band; Graphic_StripTrim lays it on the
             edge Rotation names. -->
        <edges>
          <li>0</li>
        </edges>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>
  </ThingDef>

  <!-- The corner: ONE rotatable def owning both bands of an L, which is what
       lets them join cleanly at all: two separate strips never can, since
       neither knows the other. Its two arms meet in a mitre on the diagonal.
       Facing north it covers the north and east edges, clockwise from there. -->
  <ThingDef Name="NE_TrimCornerBase" ParentName="NE_TrimBase" Abstract="True">
    <description>Two border strips joined in an L around one corner of a tile. Rotate to choose which corner. Both edges it covers stay crisp: terrain stops fading across them.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>40</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>150</WorkToBuild>
      <Beauty>1</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <costStuffCount>2</costStuffCount>
    <designatorDropdown>NE_TrimCorners</designatorDropdown>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="NeatEdges.BlocksTerrainFade">
        <!-- Two ADJACENT edges, so five directions harden: both cardinals plus
             the three diagonals they flank. North hugs N,E; east E,S; south
             S,W; west W,N. That is offset 0 and +1 in every case, the same
             arms the Corner shape draws. -->
        <edges>
          <li>0</li>
          <li>1</li>
        </edges>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>
  </ThingDef>

  <!-- The inside corner: the square a line of strips leaves open where it
       turns around an inside corner, a wall jutting into the room. Each strip
       runs its own tile's full length, so the two on the tiles beside the
       corner meet only at a point and leave a band's width of square open in
       the tile between them. This fills it, carrying both strips' bands on and
       joining them: the band square split on its diagonal, each half the band
       of the neighbour it continues. The facing names the corner as the
       corner's does: north is the north-east square.

       NO BlocksTerrainFade, on purpose. It covers a corner of its tile, not an
       edge, and that corner is already sealed by the two strips beside it:
       corner sealing closes a diagonal whose corner point a neighbour's
       hardened edge closes. The extension with an empty list would be worse
       than none, since empty means all four edges.

       Costs a fraction of a strip, being a band's width square. -->
  <ThingDef Name="NE_TrimInsideCornerBase" ParentName="NE_TrimBase" Abstract="True">
    <description>The small square that completes a border where it turns around an inside corner, such as a wall jutting into the room. Border strips each run their own tile's full length, so two meeting around an inside corner leave a notch between them; this fills it and joins them. Rotate to choose which corner. It keeps no edge crisp by itself, since the strips on either side already do.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>20</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>50</WorkToBuild>
      <Beauty>0</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <costStuffCount>1</costStuffCount>
    <designatorDropdown>NE_TrimInsideCorners</designatorDropdown>
  </ThingDef>

  <!-- The runner: both opposite edges in one piece, `| |`, or `=` turned a
       quarter. Drag it down a corridor and the floor between the rails reads
       as a runner.

       ONE def covers both orientations because the figure is 180°-symmetric:
       north is south's figure and west is east's. Each rail is an arm on its
       own edge, so each takes the strip's band for that edge and the two are
       lit as the straights beside them are.

       Two straights at DIFFERENT rotations also stack on one cell (the
       engine's identical-thing gate skips anything whose rotation differs),
       so this def exists for the ergonomics: one building instead of two,
       one drag instead of two remembered rotations, and one image owning
       both rails.

       Costs are DOUBLED against the straight on purpose: a runner is two bands
       of material, and pricing it as one would make the convenient route also
       the cheap one. -->
  <ThingDef Name="NE_TrimRunnerBase" ParentName="NE_TrimBase" Abstract="True">
    <description>A pair of border strips set into the floor along opposite edges of a tile, each running its full length. Lay a line of them down a corridor and the floor between the rails reads as a runner. Rotate to choose which pair of edges. Both edges stay crisp: terrain stops fading across them.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>80</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>300</WorkToBuild>
      <Beauty>2</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <costStuffCount>4</costStuffCount>
    <designatorDropdown>NE_TrimRunners</designatorDropdown>
    <drawStyleCategory>Defenses</drawStyleCategory>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="NeatEdges.BlocksTerrainFade">
        <!-- Two OPPOSITE edges, so six directions harden: both cardinals and
             all four diagonals. North and south both hug N,S; east and west
             both hug E,W. Offset 0 and +2, the Runner shape's arms. -->
        <edges>
          <li>0</li>
          <li>2</li>
        </edges>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>
  </ThingDef>

  <!-- The end cap: three strips in a U, closing a runner or a path one tile
       wide. The facing names the CLOSED end, so a cap facing north closes a
       run that comes up from the south.

       Three arms, mitred where the closed end meets each side. Priced as
       three straights, by the runner's rule that the convenient piece is not
       also the cheap one. -->
  <ThingDef Name="NE_TrimEndCapBase" ParentName="NE_TrimBase" Abstract="True">
    <description>Three border strips joined in a U around one tile, closing off the end of a runner or of a path one tile wide. Rotate to choose the closed end. All three edges it covers stay crisp: terrain stops fading across them.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>120</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>450</WorkToBuild>
      <Beauty>3</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <costStuffCount>6</costStuffCount>
    <designatorDropdown>NE_TrimEndCaps</designatorDropdown>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="NeatEdges.BlocksTerrainFade">
        <!-- The facing and the edges either side of it: north hugs W,N,E; east
             N,E,S; south E,S,W; west S,W,N. Offsets -1, 0 and +1, with -1
             written as 3, the EndCap shape's arms. -->
        <edges>
          <li>3</li>
          <li>0</li>
          <li>1</li>
        </edges>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>
  </ThingDef>

  <!-- The frame: a strip around all four edges, one framed tile, for a
       stepping stone or a plinth. It never rotates. Even when code sets a
       rotation (GenSpawn leaves a non-rotatable thing's rotation as given) it
       draws the same: four arms cover all four edges whatever the rotation,
       and each takes the strip's band for the edge it lands on, so the light
       cannot come from the wrong side. It drags like the straight, so a row
       or a block of framed tiles is one order. Priced as four straights. -->
  <ThingDef Name="NE_TrimFrameBase" ParentName="NE_TrimBase" Abstract="True">
    <description>A border strip around all four edges of one tile, framing it, for a stepping stone or the base of a statue. All four edges stay crisp: terrain stops fading across them.</description>
    <rotatable>false</rotatable>
    <defaultPlacingRot>North</defaultPlacingRot>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>160</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>600</WorkToBuild>
      <Beauty>4</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <costStuffCount>8</costStuffCount>
    <designatorDropdown>NE_TrimFrames</designatorDropdown>
    <drawStyleCategory>Defenses</drawStyleCategory>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="NeatEdges.BlocksTerrainFade">
        <!-- All four, written out: an empty list means the same, but four
             explicit edges say it where the next reader looks. -->
        <edges>
          <li>0</li>
          <li>1</li>
          <li>2</li>
          <li>3</li>
        </edges>
      </li>
    </modExtensions>
  </ThingDef>

  <!-- The diagonal: a border along a diagonal wall's face, for Diagonal Walls 2
       and anything drawn like it. It stands on the wall's own tile, under the
       wall (a non-edifice can be built under an edifice that allows it, as
       Diagonal Walls 2's do), with its band along the face in the open half.
       Facing north the wall fills the tile's north-west half; each rotation
       turns that clockwise, so the band always lies on the side the player
       rotated it to.

       It reads what ends at its two corners when it prints and cuts each end
       to meet it: the next diagonal of a run shares a square joint, and a
       border strip or another diagonal turning there meets it in a mitre.
       CompTrimJoins, which Neat Edges gives every trim at startup, reprints
       the neighbours when any trim is built or removed.

       It hardens its whole tile, BlocksTerrainFade with no edges listed: on
       the usual staggered build the outer row of wall covers no floor, and
       its ground creeps into the room at every joint until the room-side row
       is hardened, as rooms built with Diagonal Walls 2 showed in game. Its
       band is a tile's diagonal long, so it costs half again a straight.
       AngledLine, one of the Defenses draw styles, drags it along a diagonal. -->
  <ThingDef Name="NE_TrimDiagonalBase" ParentName="NE_TrimBase" Abstract="True">
    <description>A border strip that follows a diagonal wall, laid on the wall's own tile under the wall. Rotate to put the band on the open side. Its ends meet the next diagonal, a border strip, or a diagonal turning a corner, whichever is built beside it. Its whole tile stays crisp: terrain stops fading across any of its edges.</description>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>55</MaxHitPoints>
      <WorkToBuild>210</WorkToBuild>
      <Beauty>1</Beauty>
    </statBases>
    <costStuffCount>3</costStuffCount>
    <designatorDropdown>NE_TrimDiagonals</designatorDropdown>
    <drawStyleCategory>Defenses</drawStyleCategory>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="NeatEdges.BlocksTerrainFade" />
    </modExtensions>
  </ThingDef>

