  <!-- ===================================================================
       The vine: a vine carved in relief along a field opaque from the tile's
       edge, so it covers the edge as the floor border does; its lines are set
       in from both sides and its leaves reach past them, so on the floor's
       side its outline is leaves. Its strip holds four one-tile variants, every
       one entering and leaving a tile alike, and each tile of band draws one
       of them by a hash of where it lies, so the vine never repeats on a beat.
       The leaves and stem are a paint overlay (TrimPiece.paintOverlay): paint
       colours them and nothing else, so a wooden vine painted green is a green
       vine on a wooden border; unpainted, the whole trim is its material's
       colour.

       The defNames say Vines, not Vine, to keep existing saves intact. Saves
       store rock, ore and deep resources by a def's hash slot, and the engine
       names a Blueprint_ and a Frame_ after every buildable, sorted ahead of
       every resource, so a new trim can take a resource's slot. As
       NE_VineBorder, the inside corner's blueprint hashed two slots below
       MineableGold: on a modlist that filled the two slots between, it would
       take gold's slot, and the gold ore in a save's mountains would vanish on
       load. Rename these only after checking the new names against those
       slots.
       =================================================================== -->

  <ThingDef ParentName="NE_TrimStraightBase">
    <defName>NE_VinesBorder</defName>
    <label>vine border</label>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>NeatEdges/Trim/VineBorderStrip</texPath>
    </graphicData>
    <uiIconPath>NeatEdges/Trim/VineBorder_MenuIcon</uiIconPath>
    <uiOrder>2080</uiOrder>
    <modExtensions>
      <li Class="NeatEdges.TrimPiece">
        <shape>Straight</shape>
        <bands>3</bands>
        <variants>4</variants>
        <paintOverlay>true</paintOverlay>
      </li>
